    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's a much better way to frame it, thanks. i like the idea of calling it a \"visual language.\"\n\none more thing though—when i'm talking to my board of directors, they're a bit traditional. they tend to view the distinction between \"fine art\" (like the oil painting) and \"decorative/cultural objects\" (like the carvings) as a fundamental distinction in art history. \n\nfrom a purely academic standpoint, do you think there's a reason why Western traditions have historically been categorized as \"intellectual/conceptual\" while non-Western traditions are often relegated to \"symbolic/ancestral\"? i'm trying to understand if this distinction is based on a genuine difference in the complexity of the thought processes behind them, or if it's more about the medium and how history has written the canon.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Academically, this historical dichotomy is largely a product of Western art historical canon formation, rather than genuine complexity differences. Western traditions have often prioritized the singular, autonomous genius and the individual creator pursuing universal, philosophical ideals, which neatly aligns with the portraiture and high Renaissance narrative. Conversely, many non-Western artistic modes, which are inherently communal and iterative, have been categorized by Western critics as \"craft\" because they emphasize function, ritual, and shared knowledge over the singular, individual stroke of \"high art.\"",
      "source": "target",
